==Evolution==
Evolves into '''Dark Emperor Catdam''' at level 10.

Evolves into '''Schoolbus Nyandam''' at level 30 using [[Catfruit]] and XP after completing [[Eldritch Forces]].

==Strategy/Usage==
Li'l Nyandam is a mediocre long-ranged unit. On paper, he can be useful mainly in 4-Crown [[Stories of Legend]] stages when fighting other long-ranged attackers due to the lack of alternatives, though in practice there are many other units that are able to do the same with much more value provided, such as [[Slime Cat (Rare Cat)|Slime Cat]] and [[Red Riding Mina (Special Cat)|Awakened Mina]]. Since his standing range is higher than most backliner enemies like [[Elder Sloth]], [[THE SLOTH]], [[Master A.]], [[Professor A.|Professor A]], and [[Dark Emperor Nyandam]] (these enemies have 450~501 range compared to his 551, which makes Li'l Nyandam a relatively safe matchup), he can be used to chip away at them and provide some support with his slow ability when the frontlines get into a stalemate, though you could also be using the aforementioned two to snipe them if there are lower ranged threats for them to get a hit off.

Li'l Nyandam is of little to no use against [[Camelle]] or his variants (who have a range of 550), as his speed is far too quick, and outranging them by one makes him an unreliable counter, since a tiny shift in the frontline allows them to easily hit him.

Li'l Nyandam is outclassed by [[Nerd Cat (Super Rare Cat)|Cyberpunk Cat]] in practically every case, as despite having a similar cooldown, Cyberpunk doesn't rely on chance to slow enemies and has a much greater range. With [[Talents]] improving his Slow uptime, even one Cyberpunk can offer a lot of support, and multiple Cyberpunks can even perma-slow enemies, which Li'l Nyandam is unable to do. While Cyberpunk may have even worse survivability with zero repositioning chances, this can be patched partially with his Survive talent, the blindspot is almost negligible as you can still push back and defend better and Li'l Nyandam's very inconsistent proc chances make him nigh unusable as a practical Crowd-Controller.

Li'l Nyandam also happens to be part of multiple combos. The most significant one is [[Bad Guys (Combo)|Bad Guys]], a three-slot Research L Combo with [[Killer Cat (Rare Cat)|Killer Cat]] and [[Lasvoss (Uber Rare Cat)|Lasvoss]]<nowiki/>, which allows you to easily spam disposable units, making him worth including just for the effect it provides.

==='''True Form'''===
Schoolbus Nyandam's increased slow chance doesn't do anything due to his already horrible crowd control performance. Even with his newly buffed stats, it still doesn't fix the fact that he has terrible survivability and damage. Even on 4-Crown he is still unviable compared to superior backliners like [[Ururun Wolf (Special Cat)|Awakened Ururun]]. If you want a long range unit, [[Red Riding Mina (Special Cat)|Awakened Mina]] or even [[Miyamoku Musashi (Special Cat)|Awakened Musashi]] are generally much better choices. While both of them have less range, Mina has LD to hit and snipe enemies more consistently, and Musashi's attack rate is faster. Both of them also inflict higher offense, so there's no point of getting this True Form outside of collection.

Once [[Master of Logic Newtone (Special Cat)|Master of Logic Newtone]] is obtained, Schoolbus Nyandam becomes purely outclassed on {{4c}} due to Newtone's superior attack power and DPS.

